  "response": "Adjudication in such cases begins with a precise account of what each body of evidence can deliver, since an apparent contradiction often dissolves once both chronologies are described correctly. Raw radiocarbon measurements must be calibrated against a tree-ring-based curve to yield calendar ages. Calibration curves are derived from annual variation in atmospheric oxygen isotopes. These two accounts of the curve's foundation cannot both be true: a conversion scale anchored in dated sequences of tree rings rules out one constructed from atmospheric oxygen-isotope variation.\n\nThe stratigraphic side of the dispute demands the same scrutiny, since its authority is frequently overstated in the course of argument. Stratigraphic interpretation rests on the law of superposition. Stratigraphic position by itself yields absolute ages in calendar years. A principle that establishes no more than which deposit formed before which cannot be squared with the extraction of calendar years from position alone; the first excludes the second. Section drawings and context records document the physical relationships between layers, and an excavator asked to defend a sequence will return to them before anything else.\n\nAttention then turns to the mechanisms by which a determination can become detached from the deposit that contains it, for most reported conflicts are failures of association rather than failures of measurement. Although bioturbation can redeposit older charcoal into younger deposits, short-lived single-entity samples reduce the risk of dating residual material, a practice that arose because the old wood effect yields radiocarbon ages older than the context being dated. In the disputed sequence, at least one of two further findings holds, and perhaps both: the dated samples were contaminated by modern carbon, or the deposit was cut by a later intrusive pit. That pit is a discrete, sharply bounded truncation, unlike the slow and diffuse churning through which bioturbation works its way across a profile.\n\nFormal modelling then supplies the arbitration that connoisseurship cannot provide, because it forces every claim about sequence and every laboratory determination into a single quantitative framework. Because Bayesian chronological modelling combines stratigraphic ordering with radiocarbon likelihoods, it has given rise to statistical outlier analysis, which identifies determinations inconsistent with a stratigraphic model. That screening was completed before the site reached definitive publication, and the published outcome admits of exactly two possibilities: either the final excavation monograph revised the published date of the disputed layer, or the final excavation monograph retained the originally published date of the disputed layer; one of the two must be so, and they cannot both be.",
